rom_io: table-driven unscramble

unscramble() rebuilds two fixed bit permutations one bit at a time for every
byte it converts: 20 iterations to shuffle the address, 8 more to shuffle the
data, ~28 tests and branches per byte across 2-3 MB of wave rom (8 MB for the
JV-880 expansion). Callgrind put it at 7.3% of all instructions retired in a
short run -- all of it at startup.

Both permutations are compile-time constants, so build them into tables once
at compile time and index them. The address shuffle splits cleanly into two
10-bit halves because no source bit crosses between them: bits 0-9 of the
index only ever set bits drawn from aa[0..9], and bits 10-19 only from
aa[10..19]. That makes it two 1 KiB tables plus a 256-byte data table, 8.25
KiB total, and turns ~28 operations per byte into two loads and an or.

Measured 5.1x faster process startup on x86-64 (280ms -> 54ms including
everything else a run does), which matters for a service running with
Restart=always.

Equivalence is proved exhaustively rather than argued: see
patches/tests/unscramble_equivalence.cpp, which checks all 256 data bytes and
all 8388608 addresses against the original loops. Both match exactly. No ROMs
are needed to re-run it.

